  abstract = {Psychotherapy is the most ubiquitous form of mental health treatment and it unfolds predominantly through language. To better understand how this exchange of words bolsters mental health, we tested how the variety of emotion words used by clients and therapists (i.e., their active emotion vocabularies) related to mental health in a large set of psychotherapy transcripts (\&gt;1.2 million messages). In exploratory (N = 3,705) and validation (N = 2,485) datasets, we found that large client emotion vocabularies were linked to better client mental health between-person (i.e., across clients). Meanwhile, large therapist emotion vocabularies were linked to better client mental health within-person (i.e., across time in therapy). These findings accelerate our understanding of links between language and mental health – aligning with theoretical assumptions that using precise emotion language promotes wellbeing – and position emotion vocabularies as a potential indicator of both symptom severity and treatment mechanisms.},
